{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"AMENDMENT — my previous 'DEFINITIVE ROOT CAUSE' comment was still incomplete. There are TWO\ncompounding defects, and the mount is the SECOND-order one. Fixing only the mount would have\nchanged nothing.\n\nDEFECT 1 (primary) — the identity never resolves, so signing is skipped before the key is\never consulted.\n  reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh sets the git identity REPO-LOCALLY on the tickets clone\n  ($REVIEWBOT_TICKETS_DIR). But rebar resolves event attribution in\n  _seam.attribution_fields(), which reads git config from config.repo_root() — inside the\n  container that is /app (the rebar source clone), NOT the tickets store. Verified on the box:\n    tickets clone identity : Rebar Bot / joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com   (set correctly)\n    container GLOBAL identity: <empty>\n  With no identity at repo_root, author_email() returns '' and resolve_current_identity()\n  returns None — matching the observed envelopes exactly (author 'Unknown', author_email '',\n  author_id null).\n  _seam.py then gates signing on:   if author_id and signing_key:\n  so a null author_id SHORT-CIRCUITS signing entirely. The key is never opened. This is why\n  the events are not merely unsigned but UNATTRIBUTED, and why no 'could not sign' warning was\n  ever logged — that warning lives inside the branch that never ran.\n\nDEFECT 2 (secondary) — the key is not mounted into the container (as previously reported).\n  Confirmed inside the container: REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY is set to the HOST path\n  /opt/rebar/infra/compose/rebar-bot-signing-key, and ls of it returns No such file. The\n  review-bot has only the two gerrit_reviewbot* mounts.\n\nFIX PUSHED — Gerrit change 1567 (LLM-Review +1; Verified pending):\n  - reviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh also sets the identity with --global, so attribution resolves\n    regardless of which repo_root rebar reads. --global is right here specifically because the\n    container is dedicated to the review bot.\n  - docker-compose.yml bind-mounts the key read-only at /run/secrets/rebar-bot-signing-key and\n    sets the CONTAINER-side REBAR_IDENTITY_SIGNING_KEY in the service environment, overriding\n    the host path inherited from env_file.\n  - fetch-secrets.sh always creates the key file (empty when the SSM slot is blank), because\n    docker turns a missing bind-mount source into a DIRECTORY and would break container start;\n    the blank case now logs that the bot will write unsigned rather than passing silently.\n  - Held-out unit test on the global identity, verified RED then GREEN.\n\nDEPLOY: automatic. autodeploy.sh (rebar-autodeploy.timer, ~2 min) polls the GitHub mirror of\nmain and rebuilds the review-bot when its source changes; docker-compose.yml and\nreviewbot-ensure-tickets.sh are both in its BOT_PATHS. Note fetch-secrets.sh is NOT in\nBOT_PATHS and only runs at boot, so its change will not execute on this deploy — harmless\nhere, because the key file already exists on the host (432 bytes, 2026-08-09 15:58), so the\nnew mount has a valid source.\n\nSTILL OPEN: the 8880 existing events cannot be retroactively signed or attributed. Disposition\nto be decided (likely: document as an accepted pre-fix cohort and scope them out of\nverify-authorship)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786302520384470001,"uuid":"6f8296e2-a6f2-4990-a11e-32123f005492"}